## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to embed privacy controls into streaming pipelines and reason about masking, hashing, and lineage in a practical, production-ready setting.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time PII redaction and deviceId hashing using cryptographic functions
- Glue Schema Registry for schema versioning and evolution
- Region-scoped Parquet sinks with partitioned storage (region/date)
- Idempotent writes and late-arriving data handling via Kinesis features
- Observability using OpenTelemetry and CloudWatch for metrics and tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');

function redactPii(event) {
  const redactFields = ['email', 'phone', 'ssn', 'creditCard'];
  const redacted = { ...event };

  redactFields.forEach(field => {
    if (redacted[field]) redacted[field] = '[REDACTED]';
  });

  redacted.deviceId = crypto
    .createHash('sha256')
    .update(redacted.deviceId)
    .digest('hex');

  return redacted;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical knowledge of runtime schema validation, per-tenant partitioning, and end-to-end observability in a beginner-friendly context. It focuses on safe schema evolution, reliable error handling, and basic testing with LocalStack.

## Key Concepts
- AWS Glue Schema Registry for runtime JSON schema management
- Lambda-based validation with quality scoring logic
- S3 Parquet partitioning strategy by tenant and date
- DLQ handling for invalid records using SQS
- X-Ray tracing across producer, validation, and write stages
- LocalStack testing strategy for pipeline validation

## Code Example
```javascript
const { GlueSchemaRegistryClient, RegisterSchemaCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-glue-schema-registry");
const AWSXRay = require("aws-xray-sdk");

AWSXRay.enableAutomaticSampling();
AWSXRay.captureHTTPsGlobal(require("http"));

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const segment = AWSXRay.getSegment();
  const subsegment = segment.addNewSubsegment("validation");

  try {
    const qualityScore = computeQualityScore(event);

    if (qualityScore < 0.5 || !validateSchema(event)) {
      await sendToDLQ(event, qualityScore);
      return { statusCode: 400, body: "Invalid record" };
    }

    await writeToS3Parquet(event, qualityScore);
    return { statusCode: 200, body: "Success" };
  } finally {
    subsegment.close();
  }
};

function computeQualityScore(record) {
  const requiredFields = ["deviceId", "tenantId", "timestamp", "payload"];
  const presentFields = requiredFields.filter(f => record[f] !== undefined);
  return presentFields.length / requiredFields.length;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical privacy controls in streaming pipelines, enforcing field-level masking without breaking analytics.

## Key Concepts
- Field-level masking; deterministic tokens; per-stream salt
- Schema evolution with Glue Schema Registry
- PII handling in cross-region Kinesis→Lambda→S3
- Auditability via DynamoDB; LocalStack testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPiI(value, salt){
  if(!value) return value;
  const crypto = require('crypto');
  const h = crypto.createHash('sha256');
  h.update(String(salt));
  h.update(value);
  return h.digest('hex').slice(0, 16);
}
```

